  "statement": "Federal labor policy must establish binding boundaries on automated systems before deployment outpaces our capacity to govern them. The Accelerationist contends that freezing artificial intelligence deployment to protect legacy jobs inflicts a direct human toll by delaying cures for diseases and stopping abundance. This position captures a real moral weight, recognizing that stagnation carries its own hidden costs in unmet human potential. Yet that argument treats economic speed as an absolute good while ignoring the permanent destruction of institutional oversight when human labor is removed from the loop. The Skeptic rightly observes that companies adopt artificial intelligence to cut labor costs rather than build a wealthy society, while adoption lags capability by five to fifteen years. That pragmatic realism correctly identifies the profit motive driving the current transition, but it stops short of demanding the enforcement mechanisms required to stop systemic collapse. Both prior speakers assume that market incentives can eventually reconcile efficiency with stability if given enough time. The historical record of industrial automation shows the opposite pattern: without explicit regulatory boundaries, capital concentrates upward while communities absorb the uncompensated costs of displacement.\n\nThe Department of Labor and congressional committees must reauthorize workforce development programs with explicit mandates for enforceable human oversight. Macro-economic convergence models show rising capital returns relative to labor shares under high-automation regimes, shifting economic incentives toward labor suppression rather than shared prosperity. In the United States manufacturing sector, more than two million jobs disappeared between 2000 and 2010 due to unmitigated industrial automation without adequate worker retraining lifelines. Administrative clerical workforces now face an identical structural shock as large language models automate